: null, "tool_call_id": "toolu_01KAfPzty9LPGxc5TL5RuBap", "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "cd /hutian/w/sw && git pull -q 2>&1|tail -1; git log --oneline | head -3; git diff 9bef4c9 HEAD | head -200; forge issue view 4 -R world/story-repo -c | tail -30", "shown": {"trace": {"input": [["command", "cd /hutian/w/sw && git pull -q 2>&1|tail -1; git log --oneline | head -3; git diff 9bef4c9 HEAD | head -200; forge issue view 4 -R world/story-repo -c | tail -30"], ["timeout", "300000"]], "output": "```console\nfatal: unable to access 'http://172.17.0.1:41443/world/story-repo.wiki.git/': Failed to connect to 172.17.0.1 port 41443 after 135431 ms: Couldn't connect to server\n9bef4c9 Outline rev 4: decision rules cite PREANALYSIS rev 2 instead of copying margins\nb64ebbc Outline rev 3: decision rules follow PREANALYSIS.md, calibration findings, venue budget\n835d6ad Outline rev 2: decision rules, search-method table, figure fixes, main/appendix split\n**2. The abstract decision table has gaps, and the first results fall between its rows.**\n- The equivalence rule gives three outcomes per dataset and family (equivalent / differs / inconclusive), but the rows speak of \"all contrasts\" or \"some designs\". There is no row for \"equivalent in the datasets that are large enough, inconclusive in the small ones (NBA seasons, MT-Bench)\". The current Δ table already looks like this: Arena-55k and the single-turn subset have intervals of width about 0.05–0.08, while NBA 1948 has width about 0.28 and MT-Bench has five pairs.\n- Arena-55k flip has Δ = 0.056 with interval [0.013, 0.099]. That excludes 0 and lies inside ±0.15. Under the rule it is \"equivalent\", but \"fragility equals the null\" would be false as worded. Add a line to the table and to §3a: *within the margin, not zero*, with the size stated. The margin is a statement about practical size, so the paper should say that the difference is small, not that it is absent.\n- C2 and C3 have no abstract sentence, although they carry half of the reader's questions (\"noise or intent\", \"is a CI-separated ranking robust\"). Add rows with their outcomes before results arrive, as for C1/C4.\n\n**3. If Q ≈ 1.1 everywhere, the story's lead has to change now.**\nThe first tables show Q_real ≈ Q_null ≈ 1.1 for Arena (delete 1.11 vs 1.14, flip 1.15 vs 1.12). Then F = Q·z is z times a near-constant, and \"F collapses on z\" is close to a restatement of the first-order identity k\\*·g_max ≈ gap. It is a correct diagnostic, but it is not the finding. What a reader gets from the paper in that case:\n- the absolute counts as a function of z, N and density (Table 2), and the null-based prediction of k\\* without a search (C5);\n- the statement that the real data sit within the margin of the BT null (C4);\n- the noise comparison in equal budgets (C2) and the survival of CI-separated pairs (C3).\n\nSuggest the outline decide now that Q (and F) is introduced as the *gauge* that makes datasets comparable and as a check on the search, and that the abstract's main sentence is about counts, the null and the prediction, not about the collapse. Fig. 1 then should show observed k\\* against the null-predicted k\\* (a calibration plot, with the equivalence band as a ratio band) next to Q_real/Q_null; a panel of Q_real/Q_null alone will be a flat line near 1 and carries little. The tie-rate range (0.23–0.31 across the four data sets) and the density range (0.63 for Arena, 1.0 for the 6-model sets) are narrow; the \"applicability conditions\" part of the objective rests on the synthetic grid and the NBA/Arena-slice replicates, so say in §3 which claim uses which.\n\n**4. C6's headline comparison is in the appendix.**\nThe outline puts Table 5 (method comparison on the same conclusions, budget and cost unit) and Fig. 5 in the appendix, with only \"a short paragraph\" in main. Reviewers will ask first whether the one-shot influence top-k (the AMIP/Koh–Liang comparator) already gives the number, and whether the iterated search is needed. The answer is the paper's justification for its own method, and the venue does not require reviewers to open the appendix. Suggest a compact version of Table 5 in main (four or five rows: random, one-shot influence top-k, iterated linear+refit, exact/lower bound where available; columns: median k\\* ratio to the best found, share flipped at 0.5k\\* and k\\*), and the full table in the appendix. The page budget in §4 can take this if Fig. 2 merges into Fig. 1 as already planned.\n\n**5. Small corrections.**\n- The outline \n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "c71888cd-ff18-5169-a2e3-e32ab800d413", "at": 1795668534.1802306, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 23699, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "50d6e9c00c2f3d64083438533b1b3029", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "usage", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "context usage"}, {"id": "0ba857c2-1323-58a3-b5d7-d0248f4e199a", "at": 1795668534.2244494, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 23700, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "50d6e9c00c2f3d64083438533b1b3029", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "15d0f04f-d78e-5609-97e0-a712d1b4a894", "at": 1795668534.2280295, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 23701, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.response", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "50d6e9c00c2f3d64083438533b1b3029", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "response"}, {"id": "e31340c2-acdb-5226-82c7-c0d